`Faith must trample under foot all reason, sense, and understanding`. 7/10
So, how much faith you got there left , Sire?! ...SK2 is  a medieval village management game and castle builder with  military and politics aspects, that are governed by religion. This is a very "patient oriented" MMO strategy game that brings a good impression of the medieval times (except the USA maps which are out of place a bit). It is a game that you will love and hate in the same time. The game has a lot of potential which is not yet exploited to the max, leaving it with flaws that some will consider to much to give it a thumbs up.
In order to achieve a level of satisfaction in this game, you need to play it for a while, learn its ropes, and when a new world opens, begin your real journey. This game is not a single player crush em rampage style game, you need friends along and to become a better team you need to organize yourselves to communicate better and react faster. Along the way you will meet all kinds of players - liars, cheaters, traitors but also players who will help you at their own expense, teach you how to play, watch over your villages to protect them, enthusiastic newbies, grim veterans etc. From each one you can learn something and along the way you can try build up your team the best way possible. Then lead this group of players to victory using diplomacy and military skills. However, off the record, this game is not about getting there - being the last one standing, its more about the journey and the people you meet along. Friends and enemies alike, contribute to the heart and soul of SK2 and their desire to `colour` the map is the main fuel which this game runs on.
PROS:
- SK2 is the world’s first slow paced castle building MMO, with the sounds and some of the gameplay giving you a unique medieval feel;
- Nice overall community with interesting people from all the parts of the world;
- A large European map with actual country borders and lots of player nationality driven intrigues that keep the game interesting;
- You get a lot of satisfaction when your team manages to achieve something by working together;
- If you are a builder type of person you will have alot of fun  trying to figure out the best castle design or the best building placement in villages;
- This game really makes you think strategy. Its almost like a diplomacy simulator, you work with real people and you need to adress their needs in order to gain their trust. You can build a team of hundreds of players under the same flag.
CONS:
- Time demanding. You always need to check with the game during the day or risk loosing all your work in a few hours. So this game follows you in real life, you find yourself often doing something while wondering what is going on with your villages;
- Players learned how to bend rules to advantage and as such there are players with multi accounts who make it very difficult for straight players to prosper;
- The game interface is annoying at times.(Example: A player applies for your faction but you cannot click his name to see details about him, rather you have to close the window, open up another, then remember his name to search him... ). At times, to keep up, you will need a notebook to make addnotations.
- Pay to win, sort of. BUT... the amount of money you put in this game its totally up to you. Sure you get some short term advantages,  but in the long run its the team effort that shapes the map. Being successful in this game demands time and teamplay coordonation more than real money imo.
CONCLUSIONS
This game has  good parts, it also has some bad parts...but it deserves a chance if you are into medieval stuff and prefer politics and management over real time strategy. My recommendation: this game is free to play, installs quickly, so give it a try and see for  yourself  if you like it or not!
